			o<e_room> endif
		o<e_proj> endif
	o<e_arm> endif

	(AN ENTRY LEAD INTO METAL THAT IS ALREADY GONE IS PURE AIR TIME.)
	(With Sectioning on the ladder is computed ONCE and shared by every)
	(section window, so section N and section N-1 cut at exactly the same)
	(radii. A pass whose level the previous section already reached therefore)
	(leads in through a column that section has fully cleared: the 45 degree)
	(straight lead and the profile-angle ramp both run through air, arrive)
	(parallel to nothing, and remove no metal.)
	(greatEndian, 2026-08-31, on the 10th section of testing_15_9: *"could)
	(have skipped artificial lead in from 1-3 passes and only 4th in the part)
	(contact it should has it"* - and passes 1-3 there are exactly the three)
	(levels section 9 had already taken down.)
	(Measured with a time-ordered material simulation over the whole program:)
	(689 lead and ramp moves, 693.4 mm, cut nothing at all - 35.5% of the)
	(entire roughing feed distance - against 194 moves and 210.9 mm that do.)
	(THE TEST IS THE PREVIOUS SECTION'S DEEPEST CUT, NOT THE FLOOR. Checking)
	(the lead against the roughing floor mispredicts in both directions,)
	(because a section's ladder does not reach its own floor: on testing_15_9)
	(section 9 stopped at r33.4823 with its floor at 32.8658, so the next)
	(level down is above the floor and still cuts 0.5059 deep into the step)
	(that ladder left. What is gone is what was actually CUT, so that is what)
	(is asked, and the #2800 table below is where each window records it.)
	(A window that has cut nothing still reads 999999, so a neighbour that has)
	(not run yet keeps the lead rather than clearing it.)


	(exposed for callers doing their own disjoint-interval scanning after this)
	(call - e.g. lathe_level_next_start needs to know where THIS pass actually)
	(stopped cutting, to search for a further interval past it)
	#<_pl_level_z_end> = #<z_end>

	(EVERYTHING ABOVE THIS LINE IS THE FRONT-TO-BACK DECOMPOSITION and stays)
